<%!-- SC-10: for forum owners. What the Hub lets its operator do is fact, from how the Hub
     works; when they would is theirs (Settings → access_policy). --%>
<Layouts.app flash={@flash} title="idejo ID">
  <p><a href={~p"/account"}>← Your idejo ID</a></p>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What the Hub operator can access</h1>
  <p>
    Your forum runs on a server shared with other forums, run by the operator of this idejo Hub.
    You administer your forum inside it, as on a server of your own. This page says what the
    operator can technically reach, and when they say they would.
  </p>

  <section id="access-hub" aria-labelledby="access-hub-heading" class="space-y-2">
    <h2 id="access-hub-heading" class="text-xl font-semibold">Through the Hub</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
      <li>
        <strong>Backups.</strong>
        Your forum is backed up every day, and before each upgrade, export and deletion: full
        copies of its database (every message, private conversations included, and members'
        email addresses) and its uploaded files, kept on this server. The operator can download
        them. Copies sent off the server are encrypted to a key the operator holds.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Running the forum.</strong>
        The operator can pause and resume your forum, upgrade it, restore it from a backup
        (anything written after that backup is lost), export it, and delete it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Its secret key and email.</strong>
        The operator can reveal your forum's secret key (needed to move it to a server of your
        own). When your forum has no email settings of its own, its email goes through the
        mail server the operator set.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Figures.</strong>
        The Hub reads your forum's counts (members, topics, messages), its sizes, its memory
        use and whether it answers; not what is in it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not through the Hub.</strong>
        The Hub cannot open your forum's database while it runs, and the operator is never
        made an owner or administrator of your forum.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="access-server" aria-labelledby="access-server-heading" class="space-y-2">
    <h2 id="access-server-heading" class="text-xl font-semibold">On the server</h2>
    <p>
      Whoever administers the server can read anything stored on it, outside the Hub: your
      forum's database and files included, as with any hosting.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="access-identity" aria-labelledby="access-identity-heading" class="space-y-2">
    <h2 id="access-identity-heading" class="text-xl font-semibold">Members' idejo IDs</h2>
    <p>
      The shared sign-in (idejo ID) keeps each person's email address and names, and which
      forums they signed in to. Their passwords are never shown to the Hub or the operator.
      The operator can lock an idejo ID for abuse, which stops its sign-ins everywhere, and
      delete one when asked. As the sign-in service's administrator, the operator could
      technically change how a person signs in, and so sign in to forums as them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="access-log" aria-labelledby="access-log-heading" class="space-y-2">
    <h2 id="access-log-heading" class="text-xl font-semibold">What is recorded</h2>
    <p>
      Everything the Hub does to your forum, and every backup the operator downloads or secret
      key they reveal, is recorded in your forum's audit log, which you can read from
      <a href={~p"/account"}>your idejo ID</a>
      (Forums you own → Audit log). What someone does on the server outside the Hub is not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="access-policy" aria-labelledby="access-policy-heading" class="space-y-2">
    <h2 id="access-policy-heading" class="text-xl font-semibold">
      When this operator would access a forum
    </h2>
    <p :if={@policy == []} id="access-policy-none">
      The operator of this Hub has not said yet. Ask them before you rely on it.
    </p>
    <p :for={paragraph <- @policy} class="whitespace-pre-line">{paragraph}</p>
  </section>
</Layouts.app>
